Other important notes & tradeoffs (brief)
- Idempotency / dedupe: use client tempId stored on the saved document (msg 245/274 references). Worker checks tempId before saving to avoid duplicates if the message was requeued or resent.
- Immediate broadcast tradeoff: broadcasting to others immediately improves perceived latency, but if you do that you must handle rollback if persistence fails. Safer pattern: immediate local echo for sender + broadcast to others on confirmation; optionally broadcast pending to others if you accept occasional rollbacks.
- Queue: RabbitMQ or similar (msg 245) for high reliability; simpler setups can use a Mongo-based queue (msg 17), but RabbitMQ offers retries, DLX, parallel consumers.
- Read receipts & delivery acknowledgements: follow same pattern—clients emit messageReceived → server updates isRead in DB and emits messageUpdated/messageRead events (you already have handleMessageReceived and messageUpdated flow in msgs 476–477).
- Retries and DLQ: implement retry/backoff for worker failures; use dead-letter queue for messages that repeatedly fail.
- Monitoring & observability: track queue length, worker failures, persistence latency (measure and monitor). This ties into your latency profiling suggestions (msg 97/157) — measure end-to-end time from client send to messageConfirmed.

Why this keeps the UI smooth
- The client never waits on DB I/O — displays the message immediately (optimistic) and updates state when server confirms.
- Backend quickly offloads persistence to a queue (non-blocking) so the socket event loop stays responsive.
- Worker saves and emits confirmations; you can scale workers horizontally to keep up with persistence load.

